Fred Sloniker writes:
>Where do the names of the dieties in NetHack come from, especially the Tourist
>dieties?  (Preferably, I'd like not just a source, but a brief discussion of
>any interesting personality traits, particularly if the source is not classic
>literature or actual mythology (and thus would require me to track down a
>specific book).)

Look in pray.c.  Do a text search for Discworld.  Then you can at least 
see the minimal commenting on the source of each mythos.  Maybe someone
else can tell you more about Discworld and its deities.   

>Also, where in the NetHack source code can one find a list of all the monsters
>in the game, including ones you only see when hallucinating?  I'm coding a
>NetHack-ian chameleon on a MUCK, and would like a selection of critters to
>choose from...  (:3

Monsters are in monst.c.  You don't get a nice, concise list; you get
a monstrous structure ( :-> ) with the name of each first and then 
the relevant info.  But you can sift through it.

The bogus monsters you see only while hallucinating can be found 
in do_name.c.  Do a text search for Barney.  

BTW, info of this type can be easily found with the grep command.
If you don't use Unix, you can still find a grep for your machine
somewhere on the net.  (In DOS, Borland C has one.)  It's almost 
indispensable for finding anything in reams of code.  And a whole
lot quicker than asking the newsgroup. 

